Hello, welcome back to our course, and today we are going to talk about R Markdown reports. Alright, so why R Markdown? Well, it helps us create a document that embeds both text and code,

00:00:14.721 --> 00:00:23.721
and there are several advantages: Research becomes more reproducible, makes it easier to share analysis results together with documentation.

00:00:33.727 --> 00:00:35.727
When you knit the document, the code is executed, and the output appears alongside text. And the document renders only if there are no errors

00:00:36.565 --> 00:00:48.565
and then you can render the document to HTML, PDF, or Word. So if you are not in RStudio, so basically you can install R Markdown by using this tinytex

00:00:56.241 --> 00:00:59.241
and then rmarkdown packages. And then this pandoc is our swiss-army knife to convert files from one markup format to another.

00:00:58.824 --> 00:01:04.824
But if you are in R studio, then basically what do you need to do? You need to click file, and then you need to go

00:01:05.564 --> 00:01:16.564
to new file, and then to R markdown. And then you can see this dialog box, and you can see that you can select the title, the author, and then the output format.

00:01:17.425 --> 00:01:20.425
All right, so this is a default file, you can see that,

00:01:21.195 --> 00:01:30.195
basically, there are two chunks. There's this chunk with, the title, author, output, and date, and there are other chunks, it's like R chunk, and then there's a text.

00:01:30.213 --> 00:01:40.213
And we are going to go through all these chunks today. So, the first chunk is basically this YAML chunk. So YAML is a human-readable data format,

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:46.860
not markup, and the name is "YAML Ain't Markup Language". Originally, it was "Yet Another Markup Language".

00:01:51.582 --> 00:01:54.582
And then we have a markdown. So here, that

00:02:02.027 --> 00:02:05.027
language was defined by Gruber and Swartz. And it's a lightweight markup language with plain text formatting syntax, and can be converted to many output formats.

00:02:05.038 --> 00:02:12.038
And then we have R markdown, so this is the integration of R code into Markdown documents, and here we have a cheat sheet

